e swine flu caused by h1n1  most recently in 2009  caused approximately 151 000 to 575 000  deaths in the world and 12 469 deaths in  the united states  it doesn't take much to realize that  influenza is a global threat  however we can see  in this graph that when we compare each  pandemic caused by influenza there was a  dramatic decline in death toll across  each one  50 million deaths dropped to 1.1 million  deaths and approximately 39 years later  when the next pandemic arose  we saw that significant decrease  only 11 years later the next pandemic  hit and we also saw a slight decline in  mortality  lastly in the most recent influenza  pandemic we saw 575 000 deaths a  significant decline in the death toll by  approximately over 50 percent  we may be able to attribute the decline  in mortality to modern medicine  vaccinations and understanding of  infection control and public health  measures  the widespread exposure to influenza may  have helped as well as the majority of  people have had either exposure to  influenza or vaccinations which may help  with immunity and also lessen severity  of disease and the spread of disease  however it's important to note that even  after 2009 influenza pandemic we still  see thousands of deaths yearly in the  united states and worldwide caused by  this influenza virus  in jo in january 2011 the cdc estimated  that the 2009 h1n1 caused illness in  more than 60 million americans leading  to more than 270 000 hospitalization and  12  500 deaths  contrary to typical seasons 90 of  hospitalizations and deaths occurred in  persons younger than age 65.  so knowing how dangerous influenza is  where did it all come from  influenza is believed to exist for at  least 1500 years  some say that hippocrates described  influenza and his works although many  argue that this was not the case  it wasn't until richard shope isolated  the virus from infected pigs that  influenza was actually confirmed  prior to richard schope's discovery many  a