uses have  been determined to be reassortment of  the avian virus into human virus genetic  background  the h5n1 virus circulating between 1997  and 2007 apparently arose from multiple  resort reassortment events among avian  influenza viruses  and the 2009  h1n1 influenza pandemic emerged as a  result of triple reassortment between  avian human and swine viruses  when discussing the epidemiology of  influenza it's important to take into  consideration how the virus is  transmitted and that is mainly through  person to person  by a large droplets  and as we noted some interspecies  transmission occurs as well again  influenza a is found in humans and  animals influenza b and c mainly in  humans  influenza is a seasonal virus and occurs  more frequently in different parts of  the year  depending on your location  october to april  or may in the northern hemispheres april  to september in the southern hemispheres  and year round in tropical climates  this virus may be contagious in adults  one day before symptoms arise and even  contagious six to seven days after  symptoms begin  in children the virus may be contagious  10 or more days after symptoms begin  here we see a diagram of droplet  precautions this is the difference than  airborne precautions the distance large  droplets can travel in the air is much  less than airborne spread  once the virus enters the respiratory  tract it attaches to respiratory cells  the trachea and the bronchi when the  virus replicates these cells are  destroyed and it results in the virus  spreading and can lead to respiratory  failure  because the virus replicates in the  respiratory tract mucus saliva and  respiratory secretions can carry the  virus and spread it from person to  person the virus is typically contagious  for five to ten days as noted before  the virus's clinical presentation can  vary from mild like cold like symptoms  such as a runny nose sore throat  headache fevers  to gi illness like vomiting and diarrhea  to even more serious diseases such as  pneumonia respiratory failure septic  shock myocarditis and pericarditis  other key points to note is the  incubation period which is two days  and the resolution of fevers which  typically occurs approximately three to  four days into the infection  most symptoms resolve by day seven  however the most vulnerable population  is patients over 65 years old  super infections or infections that can  occur after or with influenza include  staph aureus strep and hemophilus  influenza  testing for the flu is relatively simple  these days there are typically two  modalities of  of testing antigen testing and molecular  testing  how specific or sensitive this test is  is a way to measure  the testing's capabilities  when something is more sensitive  it means the test has the ability to  correctly identify the patients with the  disease  when a test is specific it means that  the test has the ability to correctly  identify people without the disease  according to some studies antigen  testing is very specific but lacks  sensitivity meaning it can fail to  identify patients who are true positive  stated differently the antigen test can  rule in a disease but is poor at ruling  out the disease while molecular tests  are good for both ruling out the disease  and ruling in the disease  new technologies such as multiplex pcr  allows for testing of multiple viruses  and other potential pathogens in a  single test using a single sample  so if a patient has respiratory symptoms  consistent with a wide range of  pathogens say for example influenza rsv  cova 19 and rhinovirus a respiratory  panel testing for multiple viruses  simultaneously can aid in the diagnosis  of this infection rapidly and accurately  this provides many advantages when  compared to singleplex pcr which only  detects a single micro per sample  management of influenza depends on the  clinical scenario with most antivirals  having benefit within 48 hours of  symptoms however  we have