ng  influenza  is post-exposure prophylaxis which is  when an individual who may have been  exposed to influenza  receives antivirals soon after in order  to prevent disease progression and  possible transmission to others  contraindications to vaccines include  any type of allergies to vaccine  component  although typically rare  there is a lot of confusion as how  vaccines work and this can be a lecture  onto itself however generally speaking  the vaccine stimulates the body's immune  system so that when an actual infection  occurs the immune system is ready and  prepared to neutralize and reduce the  spread of infection subsequently  reducing the risk of transmission  the influenza vaccine is safe and  millions of americans around the world  received this vaccine for over 50 years  the benefits outweigh the risks and the  side effects of the vaccine  have been evaluated extensively and  rigorously in many studies  unfortunately a recent study has shown  that only 50 percent of medicare  recipients had claims from influenza  vaccines  meaning half the patients did not  receive a vaccine influence influenza  my final takeaway points and objectives  of this lecture  is that influence is very dangerous and  can often be overlooked especially  within the setting of cover 19.  influenza has caused millions of deaths  in the last 100 years it has led to  multiple pandemics and has the potential  to lead to even more pandemics in the  future  we have the ability to rule out  influenza along with other dangerous  viruses using advanced technology and  testing with advances in medicine we  also have many treatment options should  one get influenza  but most importantly vaccines still  remain the primary method to prevent  severe illness and the spread of disease  i want to thank everybody for their time  and i would like to address any  questions anybody may have  thank you  thank you dr frenkel for your  informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of